# lit-legs.sh --describe (plan 5.17.6, M24) prints every leg as JSON
# ({"version": 1, "script": "lit-legs.sh", "legs": [...]}) and runs nothing.
# Against a stand-in build whose llvm-lit records its arguments
# (Inputs/ci-legs.py), inside WSL from Windows (the WSL legs) and natively on
# Linux, Inputs/legs-check.py checks:
# (1) the output is JSON alone, no lit ran, and every leg and run has 5.17.6's
# shape: the derived fields (pipeline, prog_pipeline, triple, runner,
# also_run) and msil_require_verify, _exec, _link and _hosted=1 on every lit
# run; lit-legs.sh has none of lit-legs.ps1's Quick or WSL legs (its Quick leg
# hosted runs only when --leg names it), sets msil_glibc_sysroot on every lit
# run, runs msil/test/M3 in lto and .NET 10 with msil_also_run=8 in ctest;
# from M26 (plan 5.17.6) net8 holds check_msil's
# clang/test/Sema/msil-long-double-format.c and
# llvm/test/Transforms/ExpandVariadics/msil-abi.ll, programs and
# programs-lto run the nine programs and the hosted ones that exist
# (zlib-full from M26) with msil_also_run=10,10+tc0, ctest and ctest-lto
# run the whole c-testsuite directory with both lists, and ctest-lto (tag
# Exit) runs it under lto on .NET 10 against the native oracle; it has no
# programs-hosted leg (a Quick leg of lit-legs.ps1).
# (2) What --describe shows is what the runs pass: --leg net8, lto, ctest,
# hosted and (M26) programs and ctest-lto pass exactly the paths, params and
# lit arguments of their runs.
# (3) The corpus parameters print as the environment resolves them.
# (4) The failure path of Inputs/legs-check.py: each planted fault (net8
# without msil_require_hosted=1 or without msil_glibc_sysroot, an lto run
# whose prog_pipeline is obj, a leg named ctest-wsl) is reported, and so is
# each M26 fault: programs-lto without its hosted program, ctest-lto without
# its tag Exit, net8 without msil-abi.ll. The P-1a rows (plan 5.18.10 PX4,
# PX5): portable-smoke is the one leg tagged Portable, one lit run of
# msil/test/Portable with exactly obj, .NET 10, 10+tc0,
# msil_require_portable=1 and msil_glibc_sysroot=/ (no Framework, no x86);
# no other leg passes the portable triple or names msil/test/Portable; and
# check_msil (net8) holds init-msil-portable.c and msil-dotnet.c. Each P-1a
# fault is reported: portable-smoke untagged, or tagged Exit, or without
# msil_require_portable, and net8 without init-msil-portable.c; the
# lit-legs.ps1 fault portable-no-x86 is a usage error here.
# (5) The P-1b setup (plan 5.18.10, P-1b.c): portable-smoke's setup runs
# 'python3 build-builtins.py --build-dir <build> --triple
# msil64-unknown-dotnet' and then 'python3 build-libc.py --build-dir
# <build>', both logged to portable-smoke.setup.log, before its lit run;
# --describe shows neither step. With a stand-in python3 first on PATH
# (Inputs/ci-legs.py python3 DIR <builtins> <libc>): 'ok ok' makes
# --leg portable-smoke a PASS row whose calls are exactly those two, in that
# order, and whose one lit invocation passes exactly what --describe shows.
# The failure paths: build-libc.py failing (planted 'fail': exit 1; 'real',
# the real script on the stand-in build, which has no bin/clang: exit 2,
# writing nothing under the build) makes the row 'setup failed: build-libc.py
# (exit N; <setup log>)' with no lit run, and the setup log keeps
# build-builtins.py's output before build-libc.py's; build-builtins.py
# failing stops the setup before build-libc.py runs; and the calls check
# rejects synthetic calls in the other order, without the build directory or
# without build-libc.py.
# describe.test checks lit-legs.ps1 -Describe.
# REQUIRES: msil-ci-bash

# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/ci-legs.py setup %t/build

